\chapter{Peer-Review Evaluation of Exotic-Matter Astrodynamics and Topological Metric Simulation}
\label{chap:peer_review}

\section{Reviewer Summary}
The submitted work presents a comprehensive simulation framework for 
negative-mass astrodynamics, Bondi--Hermann interaction dynamics, 
Alcubierre-class metric deformation, and wormhole throat stability analysis. 
The manuscript integrates analytical physics, numerical stability methods, 
and systems-engineering validation protocols. The scope is ambitious and 
extends beyond conventional gravitational theory, proposing a unified 
computational architecture for exotic-matter regimes.

\section{Major Strengths}
\subsection{Theoretical Rigor}
The manuscript demonstrates strong alignment between simulation outputs and 
analytical baselines. The treatment of mixed-sign mass interactions is 
consistent with Bondi's original formulations, and the runaway acceleration 
symmetry is correctly derived:
\begin{equation}
    m_{i1} a_1 + m_{i2} a_2 = 0.
\end{equation}
This conservation-law consistency is a notable strength.

\subsection{Numerical Stability}
The validation suite includes Monte Carlo reproducibility tests with 
well-defined tolerances:
\begin{equation}
    |\Delta v| < 1.0 \times 10^{-7}.
\end{equation}
The reciprocal mass caching mechanism is mathematically justified and 
effectively prevents division-by-zero faults during inverted inertial 
response calculations.

\subsection{Metric Tensor Integrity}
The Alcubierre-class metric is implemented with appropriate shaping 
functions and stability criteria. The throat-collapse condition,
\begin{equation}
    \rho(r_0) > \rho_{\text{crit}},
\end{equation}
is consistent with exotic-matter wormhole literature. The inclusion of 
encrypted topology-handshake verification is innovative and enhances 
metric continuity.

\section{Major Concerns}
\subsection{Physical Realizability}
While the simulation framework is internally consistent, the physical 
realizability of negative inertial mass remains unverified experimentally. 
The manuscript would benefit from a discussion of potential experimental 
pathways or observational signatures.

\subsection{Boundary Condition Sensitivity}
The metric solver exhibits high sensitivity near the Planck-scale throat 
radius:
\begin{equation}
    r_0 \rightarrow 1.6 \times 10^{-35} \text{ m}.
\end{equation}
Although numerical stability is maintained, the physical interpretation of 
these regimes requires further clarification.

\subsection{Topology Corruption Handling}
The topology corruption tests demonstrate strong convergence rates 
($>0.98$), but the manuscript does not fully describe the failure modes 
when convergence falls below threshold. A more detailed exception-handling 
taxonomy is recommended.

\section{Minor Comments}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Figures illustrating mixed-sign mass trajectories would improve 
          clarity.
    \item The validation matrix could include additional metrics for 
          computational throughput and solver latency.
    \item The manuscript may benefit from a brief comparison to existing 
          numerical relativity frameworks.
\end{itemize}

\section{Recommendation}
The manuscript represents a significant contribution to the study of 
exotic-matter dynamics and engineered spacetime metrics. The simulation 
architecture is robust, mathematically rigorous, and systematically 
validated. Pending clarification of physical realizability and boundary 
condition sensitivity, the work is suitable for publication in a 
high-impact theoretical physics or computational astrophysics journal.

\section{Overall Evaluation}
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Scientific Merit:} High
    \item \textbf{Technical Rigor:} High
    \item \textbf{Novelty:} Exceptional
    \item \textbf{Clarity:} Strong, with minor improvements suggested
    \item \textbf{Recommendation:} Accept with minor revisions
\end{itemize}